## 5. Overlap between training and each test set

Each set is compared with the training rules of its own fold. For fold 1 the training texts and
states come from the four main training corpora; for folds 2 and 3 from their block and triplet
corpora.

{{table:overlap}}

Definitions:
- **Program:** the criteria (concept, input, operator, threshold, applicability scope, points) plus
  the combination (logic, cutoff). Option names are excluded.
- **Structure:** as in section 4.
- **Subexpression:** a single criterion with its concept, operator, threshold, scope and points.
- **Template:** a (concept, form, template index) triple.
- **Base state:** the rule plus the multiset of (concept, value, subject, status, time).

The two text columns ("Cases with a train cue word", "Cases with a train relative") count ordinary
words in the whole case text. For example, "no" and "today" occur in test-split absence lines such
as "Appetite good; no indigestion." The test lexicons are kept off the evidence lines themselves:
- the edited lines (the line a flip or near-miss adds) never contain a training cue word or a
  training relative. In test_L2, {{overlap.rule_v1/test_L2.flip_lines_with_train_cue_pct}}% of flip
  lines and {{overlap.rule_v1/test_L2.near_lines_with_train_cue_pct}}% of near-miss lines contain
  one, and the same holds for every set;
- in the other direction, {{overlap_train_side.cases_with_test_cue_pct}}% of the
  {{overlap_train_side.distinct_train_case_texts}} distinct training case texts contain a
  test-split cue word ({{overlap_train_side.test_cue_words_seen}});
- {{overlap_train_side.cases_with_test_relative_pct}}% contain a test-split relative.

Base states can repeat across splits. On rules shared with training (L0, dev, L3-alt) many base
states also occur in training: L0 has {{overlap.rule_v1/test_L0.base_states_shared}} of
{{overlap.rule_v1/test_L0.base_states}}, dev {{overlap.rule_v1/dev.base_states_shared}} of
{{overlap.rule_v1/dev.base_states}}. A rule has few distinct states, because default values come
from narrow ranges. L1, L2 and L3-inv share none. A group's cases are always in one split.

